            ("Why waves?", "On a rotating sphere the Coriolis effect gets stronger towards the poles. A parcel pushed north therefore gains spin (it is closer to the pole, where the planet's spin is larger), which makes it swing back. The result is a wave that tends to propagate westward relative to the flow (Rossby, 1939). Fast flow carries the waves eastward. For some combinations the two cancel and the wave stands still."),
            ("Troughs and ridges, weather", "Cold air flows equatorward on the western side of a trough and warm air poleward on the eastern side. At the surface, lows tend to form east of upper-level troughs and highs east of ridges."),
            ("Blocking patterns", "An 'omega block' looks like the Greek letter Ω on a map: a high between two lows. It can stay put for two weeks and produced the 2003 European heat wave, the 2010 Russian heat wave and wildfires, the 2021 heat dome and the floods in Pakistan in 2022 (a heat dome over China helped trap the rain-bearing systems)."),
